d in that way well like it's first  of all it starts from the top right you  have a leader who backed up and promoted  an anti-semitic mural you have a leader  who has talked about Jewish people not  having in English sense of irony you  have a leader who has attended a  wreath-laying ceremony for tourists do  you think you can stand on top of that  you've got a party which is it calls  disciplining someone so they've been  people who've been guilty of egregious  anti-semitism and the sanction is just  being reminded of the guide lot so do  you think that he is not a party taking  it seriously I don't know I don't know  whether he is percy anti-semitic his  actions would seem to suggest he's got  questions to answer on that front but  I've been very clear the Labour Party is  institutionally anti-semitic and you  either put your head in the sand and you  ignore it or you actually do something  about it and the failure to do something  about it just for me I just found it  made my position untenable you know my  family's being victims of racism one of  the reasons I joined the Labour Party  was because if it's as anti-racist  ideals but if that isn't matched by its  actions and I can't have anything to do  with an outfit that goes along with that  I'm Heidi Allen another member is the  independent group said that you're  probably going to be the leader  are you well we're not and we're we're  an independent group we haven't formally  met yet we'll be doing that they were  small enough in the coming week is the  tempt you know I'm clear want to play  the biggest role in this group and I  think one of the things about the way we  operate is the recognition and  recognition that we're all leaders and I  think one of the ways you're a little  bit well it's not actually because the  way you're institutionalized in the main  parties is that there's a great leader  who you clap in and out of events and  the rest and you know you worship and  everything is wonderful that's what you  hope and you just follow no I don't want  that I wouldn't want that because I  don't think that is a grown-up necessary  the most grown-up way of doing politics  and everybody's got a role to play  people may have different views great  you know let's hear that out let's work  them through and come up with policies  and we're going to try our best to be  culturally different from the existing  parties and the fact that you've got  people coming from different traditions  and we're not tribal is I think the key  difference culturally come you know of  the group compared to the establishment  parties all of them so we know what  you're against we know you were going to  Breck said it you made that pretty clear  but what is the independent group  actually for well we are a group of  independence at the moment we are not a  party and we're not a movement please do  go and look at our website the  independent group to find out more about  us we have set out we set out in detail  our values there I think look ultimately  our party system is broken you've got a  bunch of parties which are divided which  are not doing their job with the  competence people expect and not putting  the national interest first now we are  quite clear that we believe that we  should have the closest possible  relationship with the European Union  because we're internationalist we  believe that the individual should be  given a platform to succeed but we have  individual responsibilities ourselves  and people don't want their lives to be  run by the state that they need to  support education and infrastructure  health service but also we believe  ultimately work is incredibly important  value and we need to give people a lever  set a level of security that they expect  now none of the established parties are  providing the answer to those challenges  and you think what kind of policies  could what is quite a disparate group of  MPs what could policies could you unite  around specifically